Quickstart instructions for MyDNS (Docker, MySQL/MariaDB):

...............................................................................

1.	Start MyDNS with MariaDB and a demo zone (example.test.).

	$ cp .env.example .env		# optional: password, port, AXFR
	$ docker compose up -d
	$ dig @127.0.0.1 -p 5353 www.example.test

	The first start creates the tables (mydns --create-tables) and loads
	docker/demo-zone.sql. Later starts leave the database alone. To start
	over: docker compose down -v

...............................................................................

2.	The image on its own.

	$ docker build -t mydns .
	$ docker run -d -p 53:53/udp -p 53:53/tcp \
	      -e MYDNS_DB_HOST=dbhost:3306 -e MYDNS_DB_PASSWORD=secret mydns

	Without a mounted /etc/mydns.conf the entrypoint writes one from:

	  MYDNS_DB_HOST		db (host or host:port)
	  MYDNS_DB_USER		mydns
	  MYDNS_DB_PASSWORD	(empty)
	  MYDNS_DATABASE		mydns
	  MYDNS_LISTEN		*
	  MYDNS_ALLOW_TCP	yes
	  MYDNS_ALLOW_AXFR	no
	  MYDNS_ALLOW_UPDATE	no
	  MYDNS_RECURSIVE	(empty: no forwarding)
	  MYDNS_EXTRA		more lines for mydns.conf, see mydns.conf(5)

	Or mount your own: -v /path/mydns.conf:/etc/mydns.conf:ro (mode 0600,
	or mydns warns). Use "log = stderr" in it, so "docker logs" shows it.

	Other variables:

	  MYDNS_INIT_SCHEMA=1	create the tables if the SOA table is missing,
				then load /docker-entrypoint.d/*.sql once
	  MYDNS_DB_WAIT		seconds to wait for the database (60)
	  MYDNS_HEALTH_NAME	zone whose SOA the healthcheck asks for

	Arguments starting with "-" go to mydns:
	  $ docker run --rm mydns --version
	  $ docker run --rm mydns --create-tables > tables.sql

...............................................................................

The image is built for MySQL/MariaDB only: one binary supports one database
backend, chosen at build time.
